# Pharo (Smalltalk) toolchain image — builds the ALIEN-SUBSTRATE peer
# (entity-core-protocol-smalltalk) per protocol-generator/smalltalk/profile.toml.
# Mirrors the structure of containers/forth-toolchain + containers/rexx-toolchain +
# containers/tcl-toolchain + containers/cobol-toolchain (the FFI-hybrid,
# single-thread-equivalent family).
#
# ALIEN-SUBSTRATE PROBE: PURE-OBJECT / LIVE-IMAGE / MESSAGE-PASSING. Pharo is a
# live-image Smalltalk: EVERYTHING is an object, ALL control flow is
# message-sends (no keywords: `ifTrue:`, `whileTrue:`, `do:` are messages to
# Boolean/Block/Collection), and the program state is a persisted memory IMAGE,
# not a source tree compiled ahead of time. There are no primitive value types —
# an Integer, a Character, a Boolean are all first-class objects. The headline
# GENERATOR-STRESS question: can a recursive canonical-CBOR encoder + the §5
# capability chain-walk be expressed IDIOMATICALLY as message-sends over an
# object graph (a value is `EntityValue`-tagged objects, the codec is a
# double-dispatch / visitor over them), or does the generator fall into
# procedural "Smalltalk-flavored C" that reads as translated? Corroboration-first
# (the spec-discovery well is dry on the current wire surface, 25+ peers).
#
# Codec split (FFI-hybrid, the Rexx/Tcl/COBOL/Forth shape):
#   - CBOR/varint/base58/peer_id: hand-rolled pure Smalltalk (the A-005 no-lib
#     pattern, now 10th peer). Pharo integers are ARBITRARY-PRECISION BIGNUMS
#     (SmallInteger promotes to LargePositiveInteger transparently) → this is the
#     BIGNUM class (like Rexx/Python/CL/Elixir/Haskell): the full uint64 range
#     [0, 2^64-1] is carried FREE, NO fixed-width head-form self-test tax (contrast
#     Forth/Zig/C#/OCaml, the fixed-width class). Byte assembly is native
#     (ByteArray + bitShift:/bitAnd:/bitOr:). The IEEE FLOAT tower is REACHABLE
#     NATIVELY: Pharo's Float has `asIEEE64BitWord` (f64 → 64-bit Integer of the
#     raw IEEE-754 bits) and `asIEEE32BitWord` (f32), plus the inverse
#     `Float fromIEEE64Bit:` / `Float fromIEEE32Bit:`. So f32/f64 encode/decode
#     read the true IEEE bits natively (like Forth, far easier than Rexx's
#     all-decimal hand-roll). The f16 half-float leg + the shortest-float ladder
#     stay hand-rolled bit arithmetic (as everywhere) but operate on real bits.
#     SHA-256 COULD be native (the pure-Smalltalk `Cryptography` package), but for
#     a single audited crypto source + Ed448 agility we route ALL crypto through
#     the C-ABI (see next).
#   - Crypto (Ed25519 + SHA-256/384 + Ed448): NO native AUDITED Ed25519 in the
#     Pharo base image (the community routes Ed25519 through libsodium via FFI;
#     the pure-Smalltalk `Cryptography` package has SHA-256/DSA but no audited
#     Ed25519) → FFI over the C-ABI (libentitycore_codec) via Pharo's UNIFIED FFI
#     (UFFI): `self ffiCall: #( int32 ec_ed25519_sign(...) ) module: LibName`.
#     UFFI is a GENUINE IN-PROCESS FFI (the VM dlopen's the .so and marshals via
#     libffi) — the same clean in-process shape as Forth's libcc c-function; NO
#     subprocess/FIFO/co-process daemon (Rexx's A-RX-011 transport pain does NOT
#     arise). SHA-256 is taken from the SAME C-ABI (ec_sha256) so the whole crypto
#     surface has one audited source (libsodium, statically linked into the .so).
#   - Sockets: Pharo ships a `Socket` class (non-blocking BSD sockets over the VM's
#     SocketPlugin) with `waitForDataUntil:` / readable-testing — a select-equivalent
#     usable from a single green process. The peer is a SINGLE-EVENT-LOOP over one
#     green process (the COBOL/Rexx/Forth pattern): §7b store-safety is STRUCTURAL
#     (the Pharo VM runs all green processes on ONE native OS thread, cooperative
#     scheduler → no true parallel store access, no lock needed if the store is
#     touched from one process); the §6.11 handler-outbound reentry is a manual
#     correlation pump (the correlation-map tax the non-actor/non-CSP peers pay).
#
# S11: pin the toolchain + every dependency to an exact version + SHA-256.
#      fedora:43 does NOT package Pharo → the VM + image are downloaded from
#      files.pharo.org at PINNED URLs and VERIFIED by SHA-256 (the reproducibility
#      mechanism; a floating `stable`/`latest` alias is NEVER used for the pin).
#        - Pharo 13.0 image build.732 (sha e84a2d15c7), dated 2026-04-21 — well
#          over the 30-day supply-chain cool-down at authoring (2026-07-12).
#          Pharo 13.0.0 released 2025-05-24; 13 is the current stable major line.
#        - Pharo VM: the Pharo-13 stable Linux x86_64 VM artifact
#          (get-files/130/pharo-vm-Linux-x86_64-stable.zip), a FIXED build
#          last-modified 2025-11-17 (8 months old; a stable-line artifact, not a
#          rolling nightly). Pinned by URL + SHA-256.
#      libentitycore_codec statically+privately links the image's libsodium.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PIN STATUS: pinned (S11). Versions confirmed available (2026-07-12):
#   Pharo image   13.0 build.732 / sha e84a2d15c7  (2026-04-21)
#                 SHA-256 5977dac81cddae745bb4b1747b3047900fbe4d64af10bfef36d01679e1ad5a5f
#   Pharo VM      Linux-x86_64 stable (last-mod 2025-11-17)
#                 SHA-256 b4b344a8236f43f08eedad205ca6a292f85bbcbe107f5d68aed04595c43e83ed
#   libtool/gcc/cmake (image) — build the C-ABI codec (libentitycore_codec).
#   libffi        (image)     — the Pharo VM marshals UFFI call-outs via libffi.
#   libsodium     1.0.22      — statically+privately linked into
#                               libentitycore_codec.{so,a} by its CMake.
#   Pharo VM shared-lib deps (SDL2 etc. ship inside the VM zip; the VM runs
#   headless without a display via the `--headless` launch / no-window image).
# CBOR + base58 + varint are hand-rolled pure Smalltalk in-repo (no Pharo pkg
# gives canonical ECF; A-005, 10th peer to hit it). Ed25519 has no native audited
# Pharo lib → the C-ABI FFI binding is mandatory (like COBOL/Rexx/Tcl/Forth).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Build (S1 this session — verifies the substrate probes at image-build time):
#   podman build -t entity-core-keystone/pharo-toolchain:latest \
#     -f containers/pharo-toolchain/Containerfile .
# Base pinned BY DIGEST, not by tag (fedora:43, resolved 2026-08-27).
# A tag is republished in place; a digest is not. Pinning the tag meant every
# image in this tree floated on whatever the registry served that day, which is
# not reproducible for anyone pulling the repo.
FROM registry.fedoraproject.org/fedora@sha256:6e1245e9a4a72ec879b9d681ebb464a6e9fd93eea36b4506d438aceb95a7da0b

# System deps.
# UFFI needs libffi at runtime (the VM marshals call-outs via libffi).
# gcc/gcc-c++/cmake/make/pkgconfig: build the C-ABI codec (libentitycore_codec).
# libsodium-static + libsodium-devel: the C-ABI CMake STATICALLY + PRIVATELY links
#   libsodium into libentitycore_codec.{so,a}. Without these the C-ABI build FATALs.
# unzip: expand the pinned VM + image zips.
# The Pharo VM headless links a handful of system libs (freetype/fontconfig/
# libuuid/openssl); install the common set so the VM loads cleanly headless.
RUN dnf install -y \
        libffi libffi-devel \
        gcc gcc-c++ cmake make pkgconfig \
        libsodium-static libsodium-devel \
        unzip git curl ca-certificates findutils which tar \
        glibc-langpack-en \
        freetype fontconfig libuuid openssl \
    && dnf clean all

# Wire data is byte-oriented; pin a UTF-8 locale so non-ASCII text vectors + argv
# load cleanly. Pharo Strings are UTF-8-aware; the codec works over ByteArray.
ENV LANG=C.UTF-8 LC_ALL=C.UTF-8

# ── Fetch + SHA-256-VERIFY the pinned Pharo VM + image (S11). A floating
#    stable/latest alias is NEVER trusted; the exact bytes are pinned. ──
ARG PHARO_VM_URL=https://files.pharo.org/get-files/130/pharo-vm-Linux-x86_64-stable.zip
ARG PHARO_VM_SHA256=b4b344a8236f43f08eedad205ca6a292f85bbcbe107f5d68aed04595c43e83ed
ARG PHARO_IMAGE_URL=https://files.pharo.org/image/130/Pharo13.0-SNAPSHOT.build.732.sha.e84a2d15c7.arch.64bit.zip
ARG PHARO_IMAGE_SHA256=5977dac81cddae745bb4b1747b3047900fbe4d64af10bfef36d01679e1ad5a5f

WORKDIR /opt/pharo
RUN curl -fsSL -o vm.zip "$PHARO_VM_URL" \
    && echo "$PHARO_VM_SHA256  vm.zip" | sha256sum -c - \
    && unzip -q vm.zip -d vm \
    && rm -f vm.zip \
    && curl -fsSL -o image.zip "$PHARO_IMAGE_URL" \
    && echo "$PHARO_IMAGE_SHA256  image.zip" | sha256sum -c - \
    && unzip -q image.zip -d image \
    && rm -f image.zip \
    && ln -s "$(ls /opt/pharo/image/*.image | head -1)" /opt/pharo/Pharo.image

# The `pharo` launcher wraps the VM + its lib/ .so plugins. Put it on PATH.
# Headless: the SDL VM runs without a display when no window is opened (a
# `pharo <image> eval "..."` call never opens the world). We force headless via
# the launcher flag as belt-and-suspenders.
ENV PHARO_HOME=/opt/pharo
ENV PATH=/opt/pharo/vm/bin:$PATH
# The launcher finds its libs relative to bin/; make the plugin dir explicit too.
ENV LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/pharo/vm/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH

# ── Verify the Pharo pin BOOTS HEADLESS + the substrate primitives the codec
#    depends on ──
#   - the VM boots the image headless and evaluates Smalltalk from argv
#   - integers are ARBITRARY-PRECISION bignums: 2^64-1 is exact, class promotes
#     to LargePositiveInteger (the bignum class → uint64 head-form carried FREE)
#   - IEEE float bits reachable natively: `1.0 asIEEE64BitWord` = 16r3FF0000000000000
#     and `1.0 asIEEE32BitWord` = 16r3F800000 (the native-float-bits win)
#   - ByteArray + bit ops assemble a big-endian uint64 (the byte engine)
#   - blocks + recursion work (the CBOR encoder is a recursive message-send)
#   IMPORTANT (CONFIRMED at S1): the VM must be launched HEADLESS via the
#   `--headless` FLAG BEFORE the image path — `pharo --headless <image> eval "…"`.
#   Without `--headless` the Pharo 13 launcher tries to open the Morphic World and
#   FATALs with "Invalid window handle" under a no-display container. A doit signals
#   a nonzero exit by raising an Error (verified: `Error signal:` → exit 1); the
#   evaluated expression's value is printed to stdout, so each probe returns a
#   marker string and raises on any mismatch.
RUN cat > /tmp/probe.st <<'ST'
"All temporaries declared at the top (a single doit; a temp decl cannot follow a
 statement). Each check raises an Error on failure (→ nonzero exit); the doit
 returns a success marker string that the eval handler prints."
| u64max ba fact |
"--- arbitrary-precision bignum: uint64 max is exact + promotes to LargeInteger ---"
u64max := (2 raisedTo: 64) - 1.
u64max = 18446744073709551615 ifFalse: [ Error signal: 'FATAL: uint64 max not exact' ].
u64max class name = #LargePositiveInteger ifFalse: [ Error signal: 'FATAL: 2^64-1 not LargePositiveInteger (bignum class expected)' ].
"--- native IEEE float bits (f64 + f32) + inverse ---"
1.0 asIEEE64BitWord = 16r3FF0000000000000 ifFalse: [ Error signal: 'FATAL: asIEEE64BitWord not IEEE f64' ].
1.0 asIEEE32BitWord = 16r3F800000 ifFalse: [ Error signal: 'FATAL: asIEEE32BitWord not IEEE f32' ].
(Float fromIEEE64Bit: 16r3FF0000000000000) = 1.0 ifFalse: [ Error signal: 'FATAL: fromIEEE64Bit inverse' ].
"--- big-endian uint64 byte assembly into a ByteArray ---"
ba := ByteArray new: 8.
1 to: 8 do: [ :i | ba at: i put: ((u64max bitShift: (0 - (8 * (8 - i)))) bitAnd: 16rFF) ].
((ba at: 1) = 16rFF and: [ (ba at: 8) = 16rFF ]) ifFalse: [ Error signal: 'FATAL: BE uint64 assembly wrong' ].
"--- minimal-int head arithmetic sanity ---"
((256 bitShift: -8) bitAnd: 16rFF) = 1 ifFalse: [ Error signal: 'FATAL: head shift/mask' ].
"--- blocks + recursion (the CBOR encoder is a recursive message-send) ---"
fact := nil.
fact := [ :n | n > 1 ifTrue: [ n * (fact value: n - 1) ] ifFalse: [ 1 ] ].
(fact value: 5) = 120 ifFalse: [ Error signal: 'FATAL: block recursion' ].
'=== all pharo substrate probes passed (bignum uint64 + native IEEE f64/f32 + BE ByteArray + recursion) ==='
ST
RUN pharo --headless /opt/pharo/Pharo.image eval "$(cat /tmp/probe.st)" \
    && pharo --headless /opt/pharo/Pharo.image eval "Smalltalk version" \
    && rm -f /tmp/probe.st

# ── Verify the UFFI mechanism (the crypto binding path) can call a C function in
#    an external .so IN-PROCESS with the exact ec_* shape: a symbol taking borrowed
#    (ptr,len) inputs + an out-ptr, returning an int32 status. THE critical
#    FFI-hybrid probe: Pharo's UFFI dlopen's the .so and marshals via libffi
#    (contrast Rexx, whose rxfuncadd was non-functional — A-RX-005). We build a
#    tiny external libecfake.so exporting an ec_*-shaped symbol and bind it via the
#    idiomatic `self ffiCall: #( int32 ec_fakefold(void*,uint64,void*) ) module:` —
#    the EXACT mechanism used to bind libentitycore_codec's ec_* symbols at S2.
#    CONFIRMED at S1: this is a genuine in-process libffi call (status 0, correct
#    xor-fold result 0x0F), the same clean shape as Forth's libcc c-function.
RUN mkdir -p /opt/ecfakelib && cat > /tmp/ecfake.c <<'C' && \
    gcc -shared -fPIC -o /opt/ecfakelib/libecfake.so /tmp/ecfake.c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>
int32_t ec_fakefold(const uint8_t *d, size_t n, uint8_t *out) {
  uint8_t x = 0; for (size_t i=0;i<n;i++) x ^= d[i]; *out = x; return 0;
}
C
# Define a transient FFI class binding the ec_*-shaped symbol, then call it. The
# class-builder `Object << #Name` returns a ShiftClassBuilder → `install` yields the
# class; `compile:` adds the ffiCall: method. The .so is on LD_LIBRARY_PATH so the
# bare soname resolves (the same placement S2 uses for libentitycore_codec).
RUN cat > /tmp/ffi.st <<'ST'
| cls in out r |
cls := (Object << #EcFakeProbe package: 'EcProbe') install.
cls compile: 'foldOn: aByteArray len: n out: outByteArray
  ^ self ffiCall: #( int32 ec_fakefold( void* aByteArray, uint64 n, void* outByteArray ) ) module: ''libecfake.so'' '.
in := #[1 2 4 8].
out := ByteArray new: 1.
r := cls new foldOn: in len: 4 out: out.
(r = 0 and: [ (out at: 1) = 16r0F ]) ifFalse: [ Error signal: 'FATAL: UFFI ec_*-shape r=', r printString, ' out=', (out at: 1) printString ].
'UFFI in-process ec_*-shape (void*,uint64,void*)->int32 bound + called OK (status 0, xor-fold 0x0F)'
ST
RUN LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/ecfakelib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH \
      pharo --headless /opt/pharo/Pharo.image eval "$(cat /tmp/ffi.st)" \
    && rm -f /tmp/ffi.st /tmp/ecfake.c

WORKDIR /work

LABEL org.entity-core-keystone.role="pharo-toolchain"
LABEL org.entity-core-keystone.version="1"
LABEL org.entity-core-keystone.pharo-image="13.0/130-build.732-e84a2d15c7 (image reports Pharo13.1.0SNAPSHOT)"
LABEL org.entity-core-keystone.pharo-vm="Linux-x86_64-stable-20251117"
LABEL org.entity-core-keystone.experimental="pure-object-live-image-message-passing-alien-substrate-probe"

CMD ["/bin/bash"]
